SM(536,{"n":"1 Corinthians 7","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/1 Corinthians 7.mp3","d":2796.1,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.2,"Okay, so we'll go ahead and get started."],[23.36,"And uh when we get started here, in just a minute, we're gonna be picking up at the beginning of St."],[30.4,"Paul's first epistle to the Corinthians, chapter 7."],[36.96,"So, uh, because it's been a little bit, it's been a couple of weeks, uh, to sort of catch us up."],[44.0,"We're sort of in the midst of a section where Saint Paul is dealing with some issues going on, to put it politely."],[51.6,"Some issues going on at the church in Corinth."],[56.72,"We read sort of his introduction where he laid out some of his theological. themes that he's going to be talking about"],[62.36,"And in this most recent section, he's specifically dealing with issues concerning sexual morality and immorality, including a particular case where somebody needed to be excommunicated, that we talked about uh last time."],[76.52,"And so he's sort of still in that arena here where we're going to pick up in chapter seven."],[82.76,"He's going to be speaking now of the more positive end of that."],[85.72,"He's gone through what we read uh last time we talked about was mainly on the sexual immorality side with the particular case that was going on there uh that they had sort of left undisciplined."],[97.48,"They were just sort of letting this person's behavior stand."],[101.4,"And St. Paul was saying you cannot let this stand."],[105.36,"We talked about how that was not just a question, though part of it was the holiness and purity of the community, but we talked about how it was also for his sake they couldn't let it stand."],[117.12,"That it was not loving for them to allow him to continue with this lifestyle that was destroying him, and how sort of cutting him off from the community was a way of hopefully kind of shocking his attention and getting him to finally sort of turn from what he was doing and repent, since all else had pretty much failed by that point."],[142.72,"And all of this, all of these instructions that St."],[145.52,"Paul is giving now are sort of in anticipation, remember, of his visit."],[149.96,"Remember he said he's coming to visit."],[152.36,"He was sending uh St."],[153.8,"Timothy ahead of him to sort of help them get ready for his visit."],[158.2,"Uh and he was talking about how he would very much like it to be a pleasant visit, but that if it had to be an unpleasant visit where he sort of dealt with things, then that would be the way it would have to be."],[170.68,"But that he was hoping they could sort of get things in line before he came."],[175.88,"Now since there's gonna be more epistles to the Corinthians, we know that didn't really happen."],[182.6,"Continued to be issues in Corinth."],[185.32,"But the particular issue he we talked about last time, though obviously it was very serious and therefore gets dealt with sort of right up front, was not the end of the issues, as we're going to continue to see."],[198.08,"But as we pick up in chapter seven, he's now gonna be giving uh sort of positive instructions uh concerning sexual morality, specifically focusing on uh marriage, which of course is the correct arena for sexual morality."],[216.24,"So that's basically where we're picking up, unless we have any leftover questions or comments or concerns or conspiracy theories."],[227.2,"Uh we'll go ahead and uh get started at the beginning of chapter 7, verse 1."],[233.12,"Say Paul says, uh now concerning the things of which you wrote to me, it is good for a man not to touch a woman."],[239.52,"So first he says, these things he's about to talk about, they had contacted him"],[246.04,"We know he'd been in contact with different people from Corinth."],[248.92,"That's how he knew about the situation last time."],[251.88,"But apparently they've asked him some questions pertaining to marriage."],[256.52,"And so while he's talking about these issues, St."],[259.4,"Paul is now going to turn to address these questions."],[263.4,"So he starts out, is good for a man not to touch a woman."],[265.96,"Nevertheless, because of sexual immorality, let each man have his own wife and let each woman have her own husband."],[273.28,"So when he's saying it is good for a man not to touch a woman, what he really means is it's perfectly good and alrig forht a man to be. celibate, as we now have uh in the case of monasticism"],[290.4,"That uh there's nothing that compels a man or requires a man to be married."],[298.96,"Um if he is not, then he is expected to be chaste, to not touch a woman."],[307.9,"So these are the two options that St."],[309.5,"Paul sets here."],[310.64,"There's chastity, there's celibacy for a man, or there's there's getting married."],[316.32,"This is a massive change, and we talked about this some last time."],[319.68,"This is a massive change from the pagan world."],[322.08,"In the pagan world, free men, who weren't slaves, who were Roman citizens, were sort of free to indulge whatever sexual desires they had with anyone they wanted at any time they wanted."],[335.49,"Other men, women, slaves, whoever."],[338.07,"As long as they were of inferior social status."],[342.2,"Then it was it was their prerogative to do what they wanted."],[346.68,"So when St."],[348.12,"Paul says it's good for a man not to touch a woman, uh, this is a sort of revolutionary statement."],[354.6,"Women, of course, were expected to be chaste, in the ancient world."],[359.66,"In fact, whether we're talking about the Torah or just Roman law, a woman who has found when she was married not to be a virgin, it would not be a legitimate marriage."],[369.74,"And then, of course, a woman committing adultery was not acceptable."],[374.1,"So women in the pagan world were expected to sort of be chaste and sexually moral."],[380.98,"Men were not."],[383.14,"And so what St."],[384.34,"Paul is doing here that's so revolutionary, this is even going beyond what we find in the in the Torah."],[391.3,"In the Torah, all of the legislation about sexual morality is aimed at men."],[397.06,"And that's not because women can't be sexually immoral, but that's because in the Bronze Age, when the commandments of the Torah were given, men were the only ones who were allowed to make decisions in that area."],[411.46,"Women didn't have options in that regard."],[415.3,"And so it was placing strict limits on male sexuality."],[423.34,"But even within that, although the Old Testament definitely never says anything good about polygamy, polygamy was sort of allowed to go on."],[434.38,"It happened. It didn't just happen in the Old Testament period."],[438.3,"There were Jewish communities who practiced polygamy all the way up to the 10th century."],[442.54,"So the idea that marriage is between one man and one woman for their whole life really is a Christian idea."],[451.1,"This is a new Christian thing."],[452.86,"And of course, Islam likewise has polygamy officially sanctioned"],[458.22,"So this is really a Christian idea."],[461.34,"But St. Paul's even going beyond that."],[461.98,"'s He saying that a man should be just as chaste as a woman was expected to be."],[470.1,"Because he's saying here, again, the two options are do not touch a woman, be married to your wife."],[475.78,"Singular. Those are your two options."],[479.38,"Meaning on the wedding night for St."],[482.5,"Paul, both parties should be virgins."],[486.74,"Meaning that the man could not go outside that marriage to one woman any more than the woman could."],[492.9,"So this is this is uh such a revolutionary idea that even a lot of the pagans who hated Christianity and argued against it the most in the Roman Empire admitted that they admired the sexual ethics of Christians because they viewed men who were able to control their desires in this area to be almost superhuman."],[521.12,"Because of course they couldn't."],[523.12,"And so the fact that Christianity required this not only not only set this as a goal for men, but required it."],[530.24,"They that was one area where they acknowledged that that there was something going on with Christianity that was that was notable."],[540.72,"And so he also talks about marriage in terms of sexual immorality, and what he means by that is very clear."],[547.36,"It's that that while it's good, perfectly good for a man to be celibate. some men are not called to that and are not able to do that"],[557.72,"Right? And so he's not dang out laying down a law, everyone should be celibate."],[563.16,"There wouldn't be a lot of Christians today if that had happened."],[567.08,"Uh there was a group in early United States history called the Shakers that didn't believe in human reproduction."],[573.96,"And it's not coincidental that there aren't any shakers anymore."],[578.92,"Because they didn't have children and they did convert a lot of people, so they went out of existence."],[585.88,"But this is where the understanding comes in the Orthodox Church that there are some people who are called to celibacy and to that kind of life and that kind of lifestyle, and then there are other people who are called to be married, and that both of these are good."],[596.76,"And St Paul is saying if you're one of those people who's called to be married, then you shouldn't be trying to be celibate, because what's going to happen is you're going to end up falling into sexual immorality because you haven't been given the calling and the gifts to live that way your whole life."],[618.56,"So verse three, let the husband render to his wife the affection due to her, and likewise also the wife to her husband."],[626.32,"The wife does not have authority over her own body, but the husband does, and likewise the husband does not have authority over his own body, but the wife does."],[634.8,"So, two things here."],[637.68,"Again, this is something that we don't find revolutionary."],[641.56,"When we're when we're at an Orthodox wedding, we read uh Saint Paul in another epistle saying uh that wives should obey their husbands and that husbands should love their wives."],[652.2,"And when it says wives should obey their husbands, everybody starts shifting in their seat a little bit because they're modern Americans and they're nervous and they they don't want to feel like chauvinists."],[661.48,"And what we don't understand is that in the ancient world, that was not the weird part."],[668.53,"Women in the ancient world didn't have a choice but to submit to their husbands."],[672.93,"They were one step up from the slaves in the house."],[676.05,"If you were if you were a wife, you might not even be the only one, depending on the culture."],[680.87,"In the ancient world."],[682.19,"They had no choice but to obey their husbands."],[684.07,"So Saint Paul, what he was saying there was talking about how they should obey their husbands, not slavishly, that they should be, you know, kind to their husbands."],[694.83,"The idea that husbands should love their wives was the revolutionary part."],[698.83,"And I know that sounds strange to us, but that's because we've lived in a Christian culture all our lives."],[705.55,"We are surrounded by a Christian culture, we take that for granted."],[709.79,"So even our atheists have had that built into them."],[713.95,"But you find these verses, for example, in Genesis, when it talks about Isaac and Rebecca getting married, there's a verse that just says, Now Isaac loved Rebecca."],[724.11,"And we skim over that very quickly."],[726.51,"But it doesn't say that about any of the other patriarchs."],[730.59,"Right in Genesis."],[731.91,"This isn't like a common phrase."],[734.09,"This is sort of an amazing thing."],[737.07,"That he really loved his wife, who was basically a wife picked out for him by his family."],[744.19,"We have live in a very different world now where husbands and wives generally choose to get married to each other."],[751.87,"They meet each other and they both decide to marry each other."],[755.39,"That has not been the case for most of human history."],[757.63,"It was definitely not in the ancient world."],[760.71,"The people who St."],[761.91,"Paul was writing to were uh most likely married off between the ages of 13 and 15 to someone who their family chose for them, who they may not have even met before the wedding."],[774.95,"This is they had no choice in the matter."],[778.23,"And this is why there's so much in the Old Testament over and over again in Proverbs, for example, it talks it says, find love with the wife of your youth."],[787.99,"And you're like, what's that about?"],[789.35,"Well, that's talking about talking to a man and saying, that woman who your family married you off to when you were young."],[796.73,"Find love with her."],[798.01,"Because a lot of men would get older, get more established."],[801.54,"Look at whoever their family picked for them to marry and say, Well, I think I could do better than that."],[805.62,"So they'd divorce her, abandon her, and go try and find someone else, you know, who they thought was better, who they'd pick."],[813.06,"And so Proverbs is getting us a do not do this."],[817.68,"Because this was a. common, this was a common thing"],[820.76,"So the idea that not only should every husband love his wife, whether he chose to be married to her or not, however that happened, you know, whatever she looked like, whatever her personality was like, whatever, whatever."],[834.86,"Whatever history they had, that he was obligated to love her, and not just love her, but love her the way Christ loved the church and was willing to die for her."],[843.26,"That's a revolutionary idea that St."],[845.5,"Paul is talking about."],[847.18,"That's something that people thought was crazy."],[850.06,"When you read ancient literature, when you read the Odyssey, the love and the loyalty that Odysseus and his wife had for each other is sort of this epic heroic thing."],[861.8,"The fact that they that they loved each other."],[865.76,"This is ingrained in all kinds of ways into our into our culture today through Christianity."],[873.6,"For example, for most of human history, if you said to somebody, where's your home, they tell you what city they were from."],[880.46,"Say, oh, I'm from that city."],[882.38,"Now someone asks us, Where's your where's our home?"],[885.9,"And we refer to our house on the address, the place where our family is."],[890.54,"That's our home now."],[892.63,"So all of this has been reshaped."],[894.63,"But this wasn't the case in Corinth when Saint St."],[897.99,"Paul was writing."],[899.51,"Okay. This is you find this all through, I mean, Socrates had a wife."],[904.04,"She's mentioned a couple of times in Plato's dialogues."],[906.44,"But he didn't particularly care for her"],[909.26,"And she was there basically, they viewed whoever they were paired off with as youths."],[914.88,"Their wife was there so they could have some kids."],[918.0,"And that was it."],[918.8,"But that was not where love was."],[920.64,"They'd go and find love somewhere else."],[923.28,"And the the general poor view of women they had in the ancient world sort of added to that."],[929.28,"So for the Greeks, I mean, er for Aristotle, uh, women were birth defects."],[934.64,"When babies were developing in the womb, they were all sort of developing into men, and some of them didn't quite make it, and so they were born as women."],[943.6,"That's what Aristotle thought."],[945.6,"And with thinking like that, they would say, Well, how could a man love? a woman"],[952.28,"Because they're not equals."],[954.2,"You can't have real love with someone who's not who's not equal to you."],[958.62,"As a human."],[959.74,"It'll be like saying you were in love with a slave."],[962.22,"Well, you can't be in love with a slave, they're a slave."],[964.46,"And so that's only becomes possible when you have the idea that both men and women are made in the image of God."],[971.91,"When you have the idea of Christ in the church."],[974.55,"And so that's why St."],[975.91,"Paul is looking at this in a very different way."],[979.99,"And what he's talking about would really reorganize the life of Corinth."],[985.59,"When he's telling men you're not to be sleeping with anyone other than your wife singular."],[994.85,"That's it. And you're to give affection to her."],[999.49,"You're to love her, you're to be kind to her, you're to be gentle with her."],[1003.65,"This is revolutionary stuff."],[1005.97,"This is stuff that would have to change the whole city and the way it operates and the way these people live their everyday lives."],[1012.29,"Yes, sir. Um so going back when they talk when they say um because of sexual immorality that each man have his own wife, in reflection on what you were just saying, that doesn't mean that that marriage is a lesser calling because you're weak and you can't be celibate."],[1031.41,"No. But it's reflecting upon the culture at the time, and it's um and that if you're saying because of the general practice of sexual immorality. that each man have his own wife and be satisfied with his own with that one"],[1045.13,"And it's it's an encouragement against the the general practices of the culture."],[1050.59,"Not just a positioning the um the celibate versus the marriage versus first of all, he's saying that celibacy is okay."],[1059.33,"Which itself is bizarre."],[1063.55,"So he's having to argue that that's okay."],[1066.19,"We've we've now in the West gotten this whole thing that it's better somehow due to some bad Roman Catholic doctrine mainly."],[1076.35,"Uh but uh that it's that it's somehow better, but that's having to say okay."],[1080.99,"y here at all. Celibac"],[1082.03,"He's uh say Paul's."],[1090.23,"Paul says that he prefers it, but he's gonna go on to talk some more."],[1095.27,"That's here coming up in this chapter."],[1096.95,"Yeah. I think it's more has to do with like his particular field of it."],[1101.51,"But he's saying for in general, for even for people in Corinth, there may be people there who are called to live that kind of life, and he's saying that's good."],[1109.51,"That's not bad, that's not weird."],[1112.23,"That's because he then gives a command, let each man have his own wife."],[1116.31,"So he's sort of clarifying."],[1117.75,"I'm not saying you're required to get married."],[1122.0,"Um. Yes. And so then the second part that we read is that the wife does not have authority over her own body."],[1127.0,"Now he says that first"],[1130.72,"Why? He says that first because they would have agreed with that."],[1135.12,"The people in Corinth of the ancient world would have said, Well, yeah, of course she doesn't."],[1139.92,"I mean, a wife was sort of one step up from property."],[1143.94,"I mean, that was so they're like, oh yeah, of course she doesn't have authority over the body."],[1147.7,"The husband has authority over her body."],[1149.46,"It's the second part that comes after the butt."],[1153.38,"And like the after the likewise."],[1154.98,"Likewise, the husband does not have authority over his own body, but the wife does."],[1158.5,"That's the part right that was revolutionary."],[1162.18,"Again, that the ideas that were applied to women, that were applied to the wife, are also here being applied to the husband right their ideas about sexual morality in a positive sense as it concerns women he's saying yeah this needs to be true of you too also men so sure she doesn't it's not her body in the sense that she can go out and do with it what she wants right be it adultery or whatever right but the same applies to men your body man doesn't belong to you and you can go do whatever you want with it in the Roman world in particular, they viewed male sex acts pretty much."],[1206.6,"In fact, sometimes they use the same word, the way we look at like going to the bathroom or blowing your nose."],[1212.92,"They looked at it like a bodily function."],[1215.28,"And so men stopping at brothels."],[1220.65,"Or hiring prostitutes, or using slaves, it was sort of like a receptacle."],[1227.37,"And so when St."],[1229.29,"Paul adds the second part, you know, the way your wife's body belongs to you, and you don't want to share it with anybody, your body belongs to her, and you can't go doing whatever you want with it and sharing with anybody."],[1243.85,"That's why he uses the language he does, because of the way they looked at sex acts as sort of bodily functions."],[1252.97,"And so that that's what he's saying there."],[1255.43,"You belong to her just as much as she belongs to you."],[1258.63,"To the men."],[1259.43,"He goes on, do not deprive one another except with consent for a time, that you may give yourselves to fasting and prayer and come together again so that Satan does not tempt you because of your lack of self control."],[1273.55,"But I say this as a concession, not as a commandment."],[1277.31,"But we'll go on there in a second."],[1278.99,"So when he's talking about denying each other, he's talking about normal marital relations."],[1284.99,"And again, we tend to think of this."],[1289.16,"And people get edgy about this, because they're thinking, of course, that what St."],[1293.88,"Paul is saying is that whenever a man wants to be intimate, the w the wife has to do it."],[1299.72,"That's how we want to read it from our modern thing, and oh, that's horrible."],[1304.96,"But in the ancient world, that again, that was just true."],[1311.06,"In the ancient world, that they did not have a concept of rape the way we think about it."],[1318.98,"It had nothing to do with consent."],[1321.14,"Their concept of rape was that it was someone else's daughter or someone else's wife."],[1326.36,"So it was sort of a property crime."],[1328.92,"It was like theft or something, or destruction of property."],[1332.46,"It was that way of thinking about it."],[1334.7,"They did not think about it in terms of the woman having a right to say no."],[1341.1,"Again, what St."],[1342.14,"Paul is doing is is what is expand saying, okay, you as a man, you say, you know, I I feel this way."],[1347.9,"I need, you know, to do this"],[1354.87,"And so she should, well, vice versa."],[1358.32,"Vice versa. So what St."],[1360.48,"Paul is doing is he's applying mutuality to the relationship."],[1365.3,"That this works both ways."],[1367.16,"That this is something negotiated between the two spouses."],[1370.3,"That they have an equal say in this."],[1372.94,"And that it's good for their regular marital relations to go on."],[1377.52,"And he's clarifying that that bid he just said about celibacy being okay does not mean that you people who are married should try to try to both be celibate."],[1389.48,"Because as he says, if you both deny yourself that and your self-control isn't perfect, which none of our self control is, you're going to be giving yourself an opportunity for temptation."],[1402.66,"Don't try it."],[1404.2,"That's not what I'm saying."],[1405.82,"So then he says, but I say this as a concession, not as a commandment."],[1409.12,"So meaning he's saying, I'm not saying all of you must be married."],[1414.64,"It's not a commandment."],[1416.56,"For I wish that all men were even as myself, but each one has his own gift from God, one in this manner and another in that."],[1424.25,"So St. Paul himself is celibate, and he's going to talk a little more about why."],[1428.57,"But he himself is celibate."],[1430.01,"He says, Hey, I wish that everybody could be celibate as I. am"],[1433.57,"Why? Well, the reason why he's going to say later that he's celibate is so that he can dedicate his whole life and all of his time and all of his effort and all of his energy to spreading the gospel."],[1443.49,"So he's saying, I wish everybody could be like that."],[1446.29,"But he's then he doesn't go on and say, so try."],[1450.45,"Then goes on to say, but each one has his gift."],[1454.05,"One in this way, one in another way."],[1455.99,"So he sees this as the gift that God has given him."],[1458.87,"The gift that God has given you may involve you being married and being part of the community there in Corinth, raising children."],[1465.61,"All kinds of things."],[1466.99,"That that kind of life will allow for if that's the gift that God has given you."],[1473.47,"Notice again, both are gifts."],[1475.63,"It's not celibacy as a gift, and then if you don't have that gift, well then I guess you're going to be married."],[1476.75,"That's a gift too"],[1482.77,"Being able to be married."],[1489.17,"I probably don't have to tell you."],[1490.69,"We probably all know somebody who just isn't good at it."],[1495.17,"They've usually been divorced multiple times."],[1497.73,"They're just not good at being married."],[1499.82,"That God doesn't give that gift to everyone."],[1504.3,"Both are gifts."],[1506.38,"But I say to the unmarried and to the widows, it is good for them if they remain even as I am."],[1511.98,"But if they cannot exercise self-control, let them marry."],[1515.58,"For it is better to marry than to burn."],[1518.14,"And then of course they have the with passion there there is in italics because it's not in the original."],[1525.64,"So he's saying, look, if somehow you're an adult and you're not married for whatever reason, whatever happened, or if you're widowed, if your spouse has passed away, because again, pretty much everybody was married off in their teenage years."],[1538.52,"He says, if you're in that state where you're not married and you're able to, then it's good for you to stay that way."],[1548.4,"But he's saying, but if for some reason that's not good."],[1553.22,"And that's not right for you, then it's better for you to get married than to burn."],[1561.15,"And the reason the with passion or the King James burn with lust is added is because it's related to this idea of burning in heat, which is sort of used as a quasi-medical term."],[1575.75,"I don't want to go too far down this rabbit hole."],[1577.83,"But if you go and read Hippocrates, who the Hippocratic Oath is named after, he has a whole treatise on on the bodies of men and women and how they have differing levels of heat of bodily heat."],[1590.31,"And I don't think it's just based on the fact that women are always cold."],[1594.23,"Uh men are always turning on the air conditioning and women are always turning it off."],[1600.31,"Um but they didn't have air conditioning then, so I don't think it's based on that."],[1603.51,"But that's the heat the heat he's talking about is that kind of, and so it's connected to. passions or urges uh there"],[1612.83,"This uh this passage is actually quoted in the service for a second marriage in the Orthodox Church."],[1618.35,"And this is why the church in some cases allows second marriages."],[1624.67,"Because again, the ideal is for people to either be celibate or to be married for one to one person for their whole life."],[1633.17,"That's the ideal."],[1634.77,"But in a sinful world where things happen, sometimes that's not the best option."],[1641.53,"If a spouse dies and leaves the other spouse with a bunch of young children."],[1645.79,"Th areere all kinds of situations that happen where the bishop is the one who has the discretion with this."],[1652.75,"Uh, where a bishop looks at the situation and says, Yeah, in this case, we're gonna make an exception, you know, they found this other person."],[1663.15,"The best, the best solution to this imperfect situation is for them to have a second marriage."],[1671.39,"Uh, but it's based on this sort of pastoral wisdom from St."],[1675.47,"Paul. And this, I mean, this sounds like again, pretty basic stuff to us."],[1679.64,"You could either be buried or you could be celibate."],[1682.14,"I mean, these seem like pretty basic things, but we're taught he's talking to a culture that's largely pagan."],[1689.8,"Where sexuality is all mixed up with rel pagan religious rites, um, where men's sexuality doesn't really have any limits placed on it, and all of these all of these things."],[1702.56,"And so um St."],[1704.88,"Paul is here building the culture that we now take for granted, and the view of marriage and human sexuality that we now take for granted."],[1712.88,"He's uh setting it out for the for the first time."],[1717.36,"So verse 10."],[1718.32,"Now to the married I command, yet not I but the Lord."],[1722.16,"A wife is not to depart from her husband, but even if she does depart, let her remain unmarried or be reconciled to her husband, and a husband is not to divorce his wife."],[1734.0,"Okay, so this is this is extending what we know Christ said when he was asked about divorce. in the Gospels"],[1743.96,"Remember, they came and asked him, they said, Can a man divorce his wife for any reason?"],[1749.08,"And we read that like, oh, is there any reason why a man could divorce his wife?"],[1754.36,"And that's not what they were asking."],[1755.72,"They were like, Can a man divorce his wife for any reason at all?"],[1759.82,"Because that's how they viewed it."],[1761.74,"And when Christ says, No, he can't, they say, Well, but Moses said you have to just give her a writ of divorce."],[1770.06,"And Christ says, Well, that's because of your hardness of heart that Moses said that."],[1774.38,"But what that was was that was saying that a man couldn't just abandon a wife."],[1781.5,"Could just walk away and leave her and whatever children there, and abandon her."],[1786.93,"If he was going to leave, he had to give her a writ of divorce so that she could go on however she could with her life, go back to her family."],[1796.58,"Whatever, that she wouldn't just be there in this limbo where she's still married to this man but hasn't seen him in 20 years because he left and abandoned her and the children."],[1804.84,"So again, that was placing a limit on what men could do."],[1808.6,"Saying, if a man, if you're gonna do this, bad thing, you at least have to do this."],[1815.9,"And so the people talking to Christ were taking it as, oh, well, he gave us permission to just go off and abandon our family as long as we leave them a piece of paper."],[1824.08,"Which, of course, was not ever the case."],[1826.88,"But notice again how he phrases it"],[1828.53,"And so St."],[1830.05,"Paul is making that same point here."],[1833.49,"He starts with the woman."],[1834.61,"He says, A woman can't just leave her husband."],[1837.73,"Again, that was already true."],[1840.21,"They all would have accepted that."],[1842.69,"Well, of course she can't."],[1844.53,"And then St."],[1845.33,"Paul adds, the twist that St."],[1847.41,"Paul is, adds, is, and a husband is not to divorce his wife."],[1851.25,"You notice he uses the word depart for the woman, because she couldn't divorce him."],[1855.17,"So he says, Well, she can't leave."],[1856.53,"Well, of course she can't leave."],[1857.81,"Oh, and a husband can't divorce her."],[1859.65,"Wait, what? That's the that's the part that he's adding."],[1864.32,"In each case, he starts with the woman."],[1866.08,"He starts with what they'll agree with."],[1868.26,"And then he he appli appesli thates. that same standard that they all just agreed to for the woman, to the man also"],[1874.06,"But to the rest, I, not the Lord, say."],[1876.64,"So he says, Look, I'm not right now what I'm about to say, I'm not speaking on behalf of Christ, I'm not speaking on behalf of God."],[1884.32,"I'm gonna give you my opinion."],[1887.04,"If any brother has a wife who does not believe, and she is willing to live with him, let him not divorce her."],[1894.08,"And a woman who has a husband who does not believe, if he is willing to live with her, let her not divorce him."],[1900.88,"For the unbelieving husband is sanctified by the wife, and the unbelieving wife is sanctified by the husband."],[1907.44,"Otherwise, your children would be unclean, but now they are holy."],[1911.68,"So, what's going on here?"],[1914.04,"So he's saying, he's posing a hypothetical situation, which probably was not all that hypothetical."],[1920.04,"It was probably fairly common in court."],[1922.44,"Where you have two people who are married, they probably get married when they were teenagers."],[1926.84,"One of the two has become a Christian and the other one is not."],[1944.16,"Hearth in their house."],[1945.45,"Families participated in these religious rites."],[1948.41,"And St. Paul has been clear and is going to continue throughout 1 Corinthians and 2nd Corinthians to be very clear that they need to separate themselves from the paganism that's baked into everyday life in Koran."],[1961.61,"So someone might, based on that, say, Well, look, I need to, I need to get out of this family."],[1968.17,"And it didn't mean becoming a Christian did mean giving up connections with a lot of your extended family because of that."],[1974.73,"But now we're talking about a husband and a wife."],[1977.13,"So I've become a Christian, my wife hasn't."],[1980.65,"She wants to continue to practice paganism, not just wants to."],[1984.43,"But she is continuing to practice paganism."],[1986.91,"Do I have to divorce her now?"],[1988.99,"Is she like unclean?"],[1990.19,"Is what she's doing going to bring negative spiritual consequences on our family?"],[1995.23,"Or vice versa."],[1997.01,"Vice versa, the woman's in an even worse situation."],[2000.45,"And this was probably more common."],[2002.21,"A lot of the early converse Christianity were women and slaves."],[2005.61,"A woman has become a Christian, her husband is still a pagan."],[2008.65,"He's the one calling the shots in terms of what goes on in the household."],[2012.89,"He's continuing to practice paganism."],[2014.89,"Do I need to get myself out of this situation somehow?"],[2018.83,"And St. Paul is saying no."],[2020.67,"St. Paul says no."],[2021.79,"And his argument why not is that the unbelieving husband is sanctified by the wife, and the unbelieving wife is sanctified by the husband."],[2030.99,"Otherwise, your children would be unclean, but now they are holy."],[2034.03,"So what's that argument based on?"],[2036.83,"How does a husband make the wife holy, and the wife make the husband holy?"],[2041.31,"And one of either of them make the children holy?"],[2044.41,"Well, the basis of this is circumcision."],[2046.49,"circumcision. The understanding of, that St"],[2051.25,"Paul would, of course, deeply understand, having studied the Torah."],[2055.25,"Circumcision, obviously, you could only do to males."],[2057.81,"So this would be all the adult males would be circumcised."],[2062.05,"That's what made them part of the covenant."],[2064.85,"That's what made them Jewish."],[2066.37,"And then on the eighth day, male babies would be circumcised."],[2070.93,"But your daughter wouldn't be circumcised, your wife obviously wouldn't be circumcised."],[2076.45,"So what made a wife part of the covenant, the old covenant?"],[2081.33,"What made her Jewish?"],[2082.85,"She was married to a circumcised man."],[2085.01,"And what made a daughter Jewish, what made her part of her tribe, part of her clan, was that she was the daughter of, she was part of the household of this circumcised man."],[2095.45,"So the circumcised man being part of the covenant made the whole family, holy and part of the covenant."],[2102.61,"So St. Paul is taking that understanding and he's extending it to baptism."],[2108.13,"And because we baptize everyone, it works both ways."],[2112.9,"He's saying, so if the husband's been baptized or the wife's been baptized, then that makes the whole family holy."],[2121.48,"And makes the children, makes the children holy."],[2124.6,"y. So this is wh it was natural from the very beginning for Christians to baptize infants"],[2132.16,"The same reason they used to circumcise male infants."],[2135.28,"This idea of the holiness of the family."],[2143.44,"Applying that to both spouses."],[2146.0,"And applying that to Christian baptism."],[2148.48,"And so that means your spouse, even though they're not a Christian yet, isn't unholy or impure or unclean and you need to cut them off."],[2156.32,"And your children aren't unclean or impure or unholy, they're sanctified by their relationship to you."],[2165.04,"But if the unbeliever departs, so he's saying, okay, so if the one who isn't a Christian, if they leave, either just depart or divorce or however that happens, let him depart."],[2178.16,"A brother or a sister is not under bondage in such cases, but God has called us to peace."],[2183.8,"So he's saying, look, you as the Christian spouse do not leave."],[2189.08,"But if the pagan spouse wants to leave, now if it was a pagan male spouse, there wouldn't be much the wife could do about it anyway."],[2197.08,"But the other way, there was a lot that a Christian husband could do with a pagan wife if he wanted to."],[2202.86,"But St. Paul says, No, be at peace."],[2205.02,"If they want to leave, if they don't want to be part of Dazel because you're a Christian, okay."],[2210.86,"He says, For how do you know, oh wife, whether you will save your husband, or how do you know, oh husband, whether you will save your wife?"],[2216.7,"So he's saying this needs to be the way you're thinking about it."],[2219.74,"Not thinking about your own purity."],[2222.48,"And your own what you feel like you want or need. to do"],[2225.72,"But you need to be looking at that spouse who right now is not a believer, who's not one of the faithful."],[2232.53,"And so I need to do everything I can to hopefully bring them, bring them over as well."],[2240.53,"But as God is distributed to each one, as the Lord has called each one, so let him walk, and so I ordained in all the churches."],[2250.53,"So that has as he has distributed to each one, that's not going to get about the giving of gifts."],[2254.61,"So whatever gift God has given you, that's what you need to walk in, and not something else."],[2261.65,"And whatever God has called you to, that's what you need to walk in."],[2266.21,"He says, I say this in all the churches."],[2268.05,"Was anyone called while circumcised, let him not become uncircumcised."],[2272.25,"Was anyone called while uncircumcised, let him not be circumcised."],[2276.33,"So this is why all of a sudden he starts talking about circumcision."],[2279.21,"Because he already sort of was."],[2282.31,"The argument he made."],[2285.08,"And so he's now extending that."],[2290.86,"So he's saying, were you, if if you were when you came into the church, if you were Jewish, then stay Jewish."],[2299.5,"St. Paul never goes and says to Jewish Christians, hey, you need to stop eating kosher."],[2305.26,"I demand that you all start eating bacon."],[2308.11,"Like he never says that."],[2309.47,"He says, fine."],[2310.72,"Continue with your ways."],[2312.38,"I mean, there's nothing wrong with that."],[2315.2,"And then he also said, Look, if you weren't part of that, then there's no reason you have to."],[2322.1,"If you were uncircumcised, if you weren't."],[2325.06,"Circumcision is nothing, and uncircumcision is nothing, but keeping the commandments of God is what matters."],[2331.14,"Now, this is an interesting, this is an interesting sentence."],[2334.5,"Because a lot of St."],[2335.3,"Paul's opponents, of course, would have pointed out that one of God's commandments is that you should be circumcised."],[2341.96,"And he just said, Oh, yeah, circumcision isn't important."],[2344.92,"It's important that you keep God's commandments."],[2348.89,"And so when he's talking about circumcision here, he's not talking about just the one act of circumcision."],[2353.45,"He's talking about the way of life"],[2359.15,"He's talking about none of these, none of these ways of life is commanded by God for everyone."],[2366.51,"So marriage isn't commanded by God for everyone."],[2369.31,"Celibacy isn't commanded by God for everyone."],[2372.27,"Go back to the Torah."],[2374.83,"Did God say to them, hey, you need to go out to all the other nations and conquer them or convince them to get circumcised?"],[2383.71,"No. He never said that."],[2385.97,"He never said, you need to go out and stop those Gentiles from eating pork."],[2390.15,"He specifically, we talked about this back in Acts 15."],[2393.27,"He very specifically, those commandments were specifically directed. either to the children of Israel"],[2400.27,"Here are these commandments for you because of the way of life and the calling I've given you."],[2405.87,"And then there were other commandments where he said, this goes for you and Israel and everybody else."],[2412.29,"Specifically, sexual immorality, idolatry."],[2414.86,"These things were totally unacceptable for anybody."],[2418.54,"Murder, totally unacceptable for everybody."],[2421.16,"But the kosher rules, circumcision, all those things were given to the people of Israel specifically to set them apart from everybody else, because they had this special calling."],[2430.12,"And so St."],[2430.84,"Paul is here using that understanding as an example to talk about being married or being celibate."],[2437.48,"That God has called different people and different groups of people to different things, different ways of life."],[2440.2,"He didn't call everybody to be like St"],[2445.44,"John the Forerunner and go live out in the desert on locusts and honey and wear camel hair."],[2450.88,"But he called St."],[2451.76,"John to do that."],[2452.96,"He called St."],[2453.84,"Paul to a certain thing."],[2456.0,"Paul's saying, hey, I wish everybody could do what I do."],[2459.22,"But they can't, not because they're weak, but because those aren't the gifts that God has given to everybody."],[2463.62,"Those are the gifts and the calling that God has given to me in particular."],[2466.98,"And so what St."],[2467.62,"Paul is saying when he says circumcision and uncircumcision don't matter."],[2471.22,"The details of the way of life in general don't matter."],[2474.98,"Keeping the commandments of God does."],[2476.98,"And so whatever lifestyle you've been called to, wherever God has placed you, whatever gifts He's given to you, you need to obey God's commandments. within that"],[2488.06,"So Saint John the Forerunner out in the desert is called to keep God's commandments."],[2493.26,"Saint Peter as a fisherman was called to keep God's commandments."],[2498.46,"They did that in very different ways."],[2500.3,"We see this all through church history."],[2504.06,"The Emperor St."],[2504.86,"Justinian was called to keep God's commandments, but he had a very different way of life than Saint Simeon the Stylite, who was also called to keep God's commandments."],[2512.38,"It's not that there were a different set of commandments for the two of them, but they were called the different ways of life and to keep the commandments within those ways of lives."],[2520.9,"is is Is that a a Jewish perspective?"],[2524.06,"Like, could Jewish uh people accept that?"],[2526.86,"Or like from a Jewish perspective, it seems like um if somebody is gonna come and worship God part of that would be um following the commandments which would include circumcision no matter where they come from there was a it seems like that would be a net like that's that's what you have to do right if you choose to worship the God of Israel."],[2546.34,"There was a party that said that and that's what Acts 15 is about well we got to imagine Acts Yeah that's what Acts 15 was all about was about adjudicating that because there was a party that said that."],[2558.26,"Yes. And the decision was no, that's not true."],[2561.88,"That there are still different callings."],[2564.7,"And and those different callings then have different ways of life associated with them."],[2569.23,"So I'm a I'm a priest in a parish."],[2571.61,"I have to keep the same commandments you do."],[2573.53,"But there are also things about your way of life as a priest"],[2577.49,"There are rules that apply to me that quote unquote rules."],[2580.63,"Canons, however we want to look at those."],[2582.97,"There are things that apply to me that don't apply to you the same way."],[2586.51,"There are things you could do that I can't do."],[2588.91,"You can own a tavern, I cannot."],[2591.99,"Just to give a flippant example."],[2593.85,"But but I have to be more set apart from certain things than a lay person does."],[2600.59,"So there are certain and and there are uh prayers and things that I do before I serve liturgy, and a stricter fast of that kind of thing that I do before liturgy than a layperson does before liturgy, even if they're receiving the Eucharist."],[2614.69,"But that's because of the particular calling that God has given to me."],[2623.22,"So it's that same kind of idea."],[2626.5,"So yeah, there was uh a lot of that misunderstanding comes from parties in Judea who thought that uh we're the chosen people, meaning we're special, we're better, God loves us more, which is never what it was."],[2642.51,"It was they were chosen for something, to do something."],[2647.17,"And so what that this is what the prophets were communicating over and over again."],[2651.73,"They're like, no, this means you're gonna have a stricter judgment."],[2655.25,"God set you apart, gave you all this more than the other nations."],[2660.19,"Called you to this."],[2661.15,"And so when you fail to do that, that's a stricter judgment."],[2666.53,"St. Paul's gonna apply that later, later on, and not this epistle, but later on, he's gonna apply that to presbyters and deacons and teachers in the church."],[2677.25,"He's gonna say you're gonna be held to a higher standard."],[2680.75,"Because of these gifts that God has given to you, and the way you've been set apart for this, that also means you're gonna be judged more strictly."],[2689.55,"But yeah, so that's not all of them would accept that."],[2693.4,"But this is the case that St."],[2694.92,"Paul makes again and again, because it's there in the text of the Torah that these distinctions are made. where these commandments aren't applied to the other nations"],[2705.92,"And then he points to the prophets, and the prophets talk about the nations coming and worshipping Yahweh, the God of Israel."],[2712.48,"They don't talk about them becoming Jewish."],[2716.32,"It doesn't talk about them all come flooding in and getting circumcised."],[2719.86,"That's never prophesied."],[2721.94,"Or them come flooding in and giving up pork products."],[2725.32,"It doesn't talk about that."],[2726.36,"It talks about all the nations coming."],[2727.96,"It talks about there being an altar in Egypt and incense being offered in Ethiopia."],[2733.1,"There's nothing in there about them adopting those things that were given to Israel."],[2738.34,"But so that's what that's what Saint Paul is is appealing to."],[2742.26,"But yeah, there were a lot of people who didn't accept it."],[2744.66,"And they mostly didn't become Christians."],[2745.38,"didn't. Or stay Christians"],[2749.1,"Especially at the beginning of the second century, there were there was a lot of reversion from Christianity back to Judaism by Jewish Christians over things like this and astrology, believe it or not."],[2763.02,"Christians wouldn't let them practice astrology, and the Jewish community would."],[2767.5,"And so a lot of people reverted because of them."],[2771.42,"I know it seems odd, but it's absolutely true."],[2773.9,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2781.02,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2786.38,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":21.2,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: where we pick up","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Opening <b>1 Corinthians 7</b>; couple of weeks since last time</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul dealing with <b>issues at Corinth</b> - \"to put it politely\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Intro laid out theological themes; recent section = <b>sexual morality and immorality</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Last time: case needing <b>excommunication</b></li><li class=\"d1\">church let it stand - Paul: <b>cannot</b></li><li class=\"d1\">holiness of the community <b>and</b> for his sake</li><li class=\"d1\">not loving to let him continue - cutting off = hoped-for <b>shock</b> to repent, since all else had failed</li><li class=\"d0\">Instructions all in <b>anticipation of his visit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sending <b>Timothy</b> ahead; wants pleasant visit, will deal with things if not</li><li class=\"d1\">more epistles coming → issues <b>continued</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Now the <b>positive side</b>: <b>marriage</b> = right arena for sexual morality</li></ul>"},{"t":233.12,"lv":2,"h":"1 Corinthians 7:1-2 - Celibacy or one wife","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:1</b> \"now concerning the things of which you wrote to me\"</li><li class=\"d1\">they had written with <b>questions about marriage</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Good for a man not to touch a woman\" = perfectly good to be <b>celibate</b></li><li class=\"d1\">basis of <b>monasticism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">nothing <b>requires</b> a man to marry; if not married → <b>chaste</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Two options: <b>chastity</b> or <b>marriage</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:2</b> each man his own wife, each woman her own husband - <b>singular</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Both parties <b>virgins</b> on the wedding night</li></ul>"},{"t":316.32,"lv":2,"h":"Revolution against pagan norms","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Massive change</b> from pagan world</li><li class=\"d1\">free Roman men free to indulge with <b>anyone, any time</b> - men, women, slaves, if of lower status</li><li class=\"d0\">Women <b>expected chaste</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Torah or Roman law: non-virgin bride → <b>not legitimate marriage</b></li><li class=\"d1\">adultery unacceptable for women; <b>men not</b> held to it</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: man as chaste as woman - <b>revolutionary</b></li></ul>"},{"t":391.3,"lv":3,"h":"Beyond the Torah; polygamy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Even beyond the <b>Torah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">all sexual legislation aimed at <b>men</b> - Bronze Age, only men made decisions</li><li class=\"d1\">placed <b>strict limits</b> on male sexuality</li><li class=\"d0\">OT never says anything good about <b>polygamy</b> but it was allowed</li><li class=\"d1\">Jewish communities practiced it up to the <b>10th century</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Islam</b> sanctions it</li><li class=\"d0\">One man, one woman, whole life = <b>a Christian idea</b>, new</li></ul>"},{"t":492.9,"lv":3,"h":"Pagans admired Christian sexual ethics","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Pagan opponents of Christianity <b>admitted admiring</b> it</li><li class=\"d1\">men controlling desires seen as <b>almost superhuman</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christianity <b>required</b> it, not just a goal → \"something going on\"</li></ul>"},{"t":540.72,"lv":3,"h":"Marriage and sexual immorality","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Celibacy perfectly good - but <b>some not called</b> to it</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul not laying down law: <b>everyone celibate</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"there wouldn't be a lot of Christians today\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Shakers</b> - no reproduction → no Shakers left</li><li class=\"d0\">Orthodox view: some called to celibacy, others to marriage - <b>both good</b></li><li class=\"d0\">If called to marriage and try celibacy → fall into <b>immorality</b> - not given the calling and gifts</li></ul>"},{"t":618.56,"lv":2,"h":"7:3-4 - Mutual authority over the body","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:3-4</b> husband renders <b>affection due</b>; wife no authority over her body, husband does - <b>likewise</b> husband</li><li class=\"d0\">Orthodox wedding: wives obey husbands, husbands love wives → people shift in seats</li><li class=\"d1\">in ancient world <b>obey</b> was not the weird part</li><li class=\"d1\">wives had no choice; \"one step up from the slaves\"; maybe not the only wife</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: not slavishly - <b>be kind</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>husbands should love their wives</b> = the revolutionary part</li><li class=\"d0\">We take it for granted - <b>even our atheists</b> have it built in</li></ul>"},{"t":713.95,"lv":3,"h":"Husbands loving wives","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Genesis: \"Now <b>Isaac loved Rebecca</b>\" - said of no other patriarch</li><li class=\"d1\">wife picked by his family - amazing</li><li class=\"d0\">Today: husbands and wives <b>choose each other</b> - not most of history</li><li class=\"d0\">Corinthians married off at <b>13-15</b>, maybe never met before the wedding</li><li class=\"d0\">Proverbs: \"find love with the <b>wife of your youth</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">men got older, established → <b>divorce, abandon</b>, find someone they picked</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: love her whatever she looked like, whatever her personality, history</li><li class=\"d1\">love as <b>Christ loved the church</b>, willing to die for her</li><li class=\"d1\">people thought it <b>crazy</b></li></ul>"},{"t":850.06,"lv":2,"h":"Ancient views of women and love","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Odyssey</b>: Odysseus and wife's loyalty = epic, heroic thing</li><li class=\"d0\">Ingrained in our culture via Christianity</li><li class=\"d1\">ancient \"home\" = <b>your city</b>; now = house where family is</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Socrates</b> had a wife, didn't care for her</li><li class=\"d1\">wife there for <b>kids</b>; love found elsewhere</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Aristotle</b>: women = <b>birth defects</b>, men who \"didn't make it\"</li><li class=\"d1\">can't love an unequal - \"like being in love with a slave\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Possible only with <b>image of God</b> in both, <b>Christ and the church</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's teaching would <b>reorganize Corinth</b></li><li class=\"d1\">one wife only; affection, kindness, gentleness</li></ul>"},{"t":1012.29,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: marriage a lesser calling?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: \"because of sexual immorality\" - doesn't mean marriage is lesser, for the weak?</li><li class=\"d0\">A: <b>No</b> - reflects the culture; encouragement <b>against</b> general practice</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul first saying <b>celibacy is okay</b> - itself \"bizarre\"</li><li class=\"d1\">West: celibacy \"somehow better\" - \"bad Roman Catholic doctrine mainly\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul says he <b>prefers</b> it - more coming this chapter</li><li class=\"d0\">For Corinthians some called to celibacy - <b>good, not weird</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Then command: <b>each man his own wife</b> - not required to marry</li></ul>"},{"t":1122.0,"lv":2,"h":"Her body, his body","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Wife's authority over body said <b>first</b> - they'd agree: wife = \"one step up from property\"</li><li class=\"d0\">The <b>likewise</b> = revolutionary: husband's body not his own either</li><li class=\"d1\">same ideas applied to <b>men</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Romans: male sex acts = <b>bodily function</b>, like going to the bathroom or blowing your nose</li><li class=\"d1\">brothels, prostitutes, slaves = \"a receptacle\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: your body <b>belongs to her</b>, can't share with anybody</li></ul>"},{"t":1259.43,"lv":2,"h":"7:5-7 - Do not deprive one another","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:5</b> do not deprive except by <b>consent</b>, for a time, for <b>fasting and prayer</b> - then come together lest <b>Satan tempts</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not \"whenever he wants, she must\"</li><li class=\"d1\">ancient world: no concept of <b>rape</b> as consent - <b>property crime</b>, someone's daughter or wife</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul applies <b>mutuality</b></li><li class=\"d1\">negotiated, <b>equal say</b>, works both ways</li><li class=\"d0\">Celibacy okay ≠ married couples both celibate</li><li class=\"d1\">self-control never perfect → \"<b>Don't try it</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1405.82,"lv":3,"h":"7:6-7 - Concession, and gifts","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:6</b> \"a concession, not a commandment\" - <b>not all must be married</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:7</b> \"I wish all men were even as myself\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul <b>celibate</b> - later: to give <b>all his time and energy to the gospel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't say \"so try\" - <b>each has his own gift</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Both are gifts</b> - marriage too</li><li class=\"d1\">some people \"just aren't good at it\" - divorced repeatedly - God doesn't give it to everyone</li></ul>"},{"t":1506.38,"lv":2,"h":"7:8-9 - Better to marry than burn","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:8-9</b> unmarried and widows: good to remain as I am; if no self-control, <b>marry</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"it is better to marry than to burn\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Most were married off as teens → unmarried adult = widow or similar</li><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>with passion</b>\" is italicized - not in original</li><li class=\"d1\">burning = heat, <b>quasi-medical</b> term</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Hippocrates</b> treatise: bodies of men and women, differing levels of heat</li><li class=\"d1\">\"men always turning on the air conditioning\" - but they had none then</li></ul>"},{"t":1612.83,"lv":3,"h":"Second marriages in the Church","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Passage quoted in the <b>service for a second marriage</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ideal: <b>celibate</b> or married to <b>one person for life</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sinful world → sometimes not the best option</li><li class=\"d1\">e.g. spouse dies, leaves young children</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Bishop</b> has discretion → exception, <b>second marriage</b> best solution to imperfect situation</li><li class=\"d0\">Based on Paul's <b>pastoral wisdom</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>building the culture</b> we take for granted - sets it out for the first time</li></ul>"},{"t":1717.36,"lv":2,"h":"7:10-11 - Divorce","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:10-11</b> \"I command, yet not I but the <b>Lord</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">wife not to <b>depart</b>; if she does, remain <b>unmarried</b> or be reconciled</li><li class=\"d1\">husband not to <b>divorce</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Extends Christ's answer in the Gospels</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Can a man divorce his wife <b>for any reason</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ: Moses allowed it because of <b>hardness of heart</b></li><li class=\"d1\">writ of divorce = limit: couldn't <b>abandon</b> wife and children, leave her in limbo for 20 years</li><li class=\"d1\">they heard it as permission - never the case</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul starts with the woman (they'd agree), then <b>applies the same to the man</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"depart\" for her - she couldn't divorce him</li></ul>"},{"t":1874.06,"lv":2,"h":"7:12-16 - Believer married to unbeliever","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:12</b> \"to the rest, I, <b>not the Lord</b>\" = his opinion</li><li class=\"d1\">brother with unbelieving wife willing to stay: <b>don't divorce</b>; same for woman</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:14</b> unbeliever <b>sanctified</b> by spouse; else children <b>unclean</b>, now holy</li><li class=\"d0\">Probably common: married as teens, <b>one converts</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paganism baked into daily life; Paul: <b>separate</b> from it</li><li class=\"d1\">question: unclean? spiritual consequences for family?</li><li class=\"d1\">early converts often <b>women and slaves</b> - husband calls the shots</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: <b>no</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2034.03,"lv":3,"h":"Basis: circumcision, now baptism","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">How does a spouse make the other holy? → <b>circumcision</b></li><li class=\"d1\">only males circumcised: adults, babies on <b>8th day</b></li><li class=\"d1\">wife part of covenant by marriage to a circumcised man; daughter by <b>household</b></li><li class=\"d1\">circumcised man → <b>whole family holy</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul extends to <b>baptism</b> - works both ways</li><li class=\"d0\">→ natural from the start to <b>baptize infants</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Unbelieving spouse not unclean/impure; children <b>sanctified by relationship to you</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2165.04,"lv":3,"h":"7:15-16 - If the unbeliever departs","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:15</b> if unbeliever departs, <b>let him depart</b>; \"not under bondage\"; God called us to <b>peace</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christian spouse doesn't leave</li><li class=\"d1\">pagan husband leaving - wife could do little; Christian husband could do a lot to pagan wife - Paul: <b>be at peace</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:16</b> \"how do you know whether you will <b>save</b> your husband?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">think of the spouse, not your own purity or wants - do everything to <b>bring them over</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2240.53,"lv":2,"h":"7:17-20 - Remain in your calling","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:17</b> as God distributed, as called, so walk - \"I ordain in <b>all the churches</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">gifts and calling - walk in them, not something else</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:18-19</b> circumcised: don't become uncircumcised, and vice versa</li><li class=\"d1\">Jewish Christians: Paul never says \"stop eating <b>kosher</b>, eat bacon\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"circumcision is nothing... keeping the <b>commandments of God</b> is what matters\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Opponents: circumcision <b>is</b> a commandment</li></ul>"},{"t":2331.14,"lv":2,"h":"Commandments versus ways of life","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not about one act - the <b>way of life</b></li><li class=\"d0\">None commanded for everyone: <b>marriage, celibacy</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Torah: God never told Israel to make <b>Gentiles</b> circumcised or stop eating pork - <b>Acts 15</b></li><li class=\"d1\">some commandments for <b>everybody</b>: sexual immorality, idolatry, murder</li><li class=\"d1\">kosher, circumcision: Israel only, to <b>set them apart</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Different callings, different ways of life</li><li class=\"d1\">not everyone to be <b>John the Forerunner</b> - locusts, camel hair</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not because they're weak\" - gifts given to Paul in particular</li><li class=\"d0\">Keep the commandments <b>within</b> your way of life</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>John</b> in desert, <b>Peter</b> as fisherman</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Justinian</b> vs <b>Simeon the Stylite</b> - same commandments, different lives</li></ul>"},{"t":2520.9,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: Jewish perspective on circumcision","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: could Jews accept this? Worshipping God means following commandments incl. circumcision</li><li class=\"d0\">A: there was a <b>party</b> that said that - what <b>Acts 15</b> adjudicated</li><li class=\"d1\">decision: no - different callings, different ways of life</li></ul>"},{"t":2569.23,"lv":3,"h":"Example: the priest's calling","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Priest keeps the same commandments as laity</li><li class=\"d1\">but canons/\"rules\" for him <b>don't apply the same</b> to you</li><li class=\"d1\">can't <b>own a tavern</b> - \"flippant example\"</li><li class=\"d1\">prayers and <b>stricter fast</b> before liturgy, even vs laity receiving the Eucharist</li><li class=\"d1\">because of his <b>particular calling</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2626.5,"lv":3,"h":"Chosen for something, not better","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Judean parties: \"chosen = special, better, God loves us more\" - <b>never</b> what it was</li><li class=\"d1\">chosen <b>to do something</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Prophets: <b>stricter judgment</b> for failing the calling</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul later: presbyters, deacons, teachers <b>held to higher standard</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2705.92,"lv":2,"h":"Prophets, the nations, and reversion","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Torah text itself distinguishes - commandments not applied to other nations</li><li class=\"d0\">Prophets: nations come and <b>worship Yahweh</b> - not become Jewish</li><li class=\"d1\">never prophesied: flooding in to be <b>circumcised</b> or give up pork</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>altar in Egypt</b>, incense in <b>Ethiopia</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Many Jews didn't accept it - didn't become or stay Christian</li><li class=\"d1\">early <b>2nd century</b>: lots of reversion to Judaism by Jewish Christians</li><li class=\"d1\">over such things and <b>astrology</b> - Christians wouldn't allow it, Jewish community would</li></ul>"}]});
